course: dictionary
lesson: 5125
title: Invent: make new, or make up
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Invent has two meanings, and the context tells you which one. Read the words around it, then ask: is someone making something new, or making something up that is not true? «In the late 1950s engineers invented the microchip» is about making something that did not exist before.
explain: Now read «Asked who made you, you can say you are an experimental small model... without inventing details.» Here invent means making up details that are not true. The clues are «without» and «details»: the sentence says do not make things up. It is the same word with a different meaning.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Could the engineers have made up the microchip? No, so the first meaning fits there. If you can replace invent with design or create and the sentence still makes sense, it is the new-thing meaning. If you would say made up or pretended instead, it is the untrue meaning.
explain: The word changes its form with time: invent, invents, invented, inventing. Invented is the past form, so «The World Wide Web was invented by Tim Berners-Lee in 1989» tells you when something happened, and that something is new.
fact: Invent means to make or design something new that did not exist before.
fact: Invent can also mean to make up a story or excuse that is not true.
fact: «In the late 1950s engineers invented the microchip, which put many tiny parts onto a small piece of silicon.»
fact: «The World Wide Web was invented by Tim Berners-Lee in 1989.»
fact: An invention is a new thing that solves a problem, such as the light bulb, the telephone or the bicycle.
exercise: «In the late 1950s engineers invented the microchip.» What does invented mean here? | Lost something in a cupboard | *Made something new that did not exist before | Bought it in a shop | Made up an untrue story
exercise: «Without inventing details, you can say you are a small model made by people.» What does inventing mean here? | *Making up things that are not true | Telling a joke with a funny ending | Copying a page from a book | Designing a brand new machine
exercise: «The World Wide Web was invented by Tim Berners-Lee in 1989.» Find and copy the past form of invent. | *invented | invent | invents | inventing
test: Priya says, «My brother invented an excuse for being late.» What does invented mean in her sentence? | *Made up something that is not true | Practised a song for the school show | Made a new machine | Drew a picture of a clock
test: Mia asks, «Does invent always mean make something new?» What is the answer? | No, it only means to copy | Yes, but only for machines | *No, it can also mean to make up something that is not true | Yes, it never means anything else
exam: «Writing was invented separately in Mesopotamia about 5,200 years ago.» What does invented mean here? | Made up a story that was not true | Forgot how to do something | Copied it from a neighbour | *Made something new that did not exist before
